<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neumark</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New March</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neumark ('New March') was the part of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> east of the Oder, acquired by the Ascanian margraves in the 13th century. Sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1402, it was bought back by Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II</a> in 1455. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and its chief town, Landsberg an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warthe</a>, is now Gorzów Wielkopolski.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Neumark, also called East Brandenburg, was bounded by the Oder to the west and south, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> to the north and by Greater Poland to the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> and Noteć rivers and their marshes dominated its landscape. Its towns included Landsberg, Soldin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a>, Arnswalde, Friedeberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in the New March, and it long remained a land of agriculture and forestry, its middling towns mostly farmer-citizen towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 10th century the area came under the first Polish state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> and Bolesław I and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Lubusz Land</a>. From the 1230s Low German colonists settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> and Noteć at the invitation of Pomeranian and Polish lords. The Ascanian margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> had a foothold east of the Oder by 1242, bought the Lubusz Land with the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in 1252, and extended their hold through purchases and marriages; Margrave John I founded Landsberg an der Warthe in 1257. The land across the Oder came to be called the New March after the middle of the 15th century.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">Ascanians</a> died out in 1320 the region was neglected under the Wittelsbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> margraves, as it later was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. From 1535 to 1571 it was the separate margraviate of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a> under John, a younger son of Elector Joachim I Nestor, who made it Lutheran and moved his seat to Küstrin in 1548. In the Thirty Years' War Swedish and imperial troops ravaged it, and plague struck in 1626 and 1631. Under the Kingdom of Prussia Huguenot settlers and textile industry came, Frederick II drained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> and Noteć lowlands, and after 1815 most of it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-brandenburg">Province of Brandenburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1939 the Neumark had 645,000 inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> reached it at the end of January 1945, and civilians who had not fled in time suffered greatly. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> terms the region was placed under Polish administration and its Germans were expelled; by 1950 over 95 per cent of the people were newcomers, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> expelled from the eastern lands annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Since 1999 almost all of the former Neumark lies in the Lubusz Voivodeship, while villages west of the regulated Oder remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neumark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His Ascanian successors pushed east through the Havelland, Teltow and Barnim and beyond the Oder into the <b>Neumark</b>, bringing in German and Flemish settlers, founding Cistercian monasteries such as Lehnin in 1180, and chartering towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 1535 his elder son Joachim II became elector of the Margraviate of Brandenburg, while the younger, John, received the <b>Neumark</b> east of the Oder, with Crossen and other districts, ruling from Küstrin.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-brandenburg-kustrin">Margraviate of Brandenburg-Küstrin</a></li></ul></section>
